{=============================================================================
  Near-supercritical internal convection: Nusselt-number correlations for
  vertical upward pipe flow
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0104 (function library, kind = function)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Eighteen Nusselt-number correlations for turbulent vertical upward flow in a
  pipe at near-supercritical pressure, from McAdams (1942) to
  Krasnoshchekov-Protopopov (1959) and Petukhov-Kurganov-Ankudinov (1983):
  McAdams, Shitsman, Griem, Jackson, Gupta, Swenson, Xu, Mokry, Bringer-Smith,
  Ornatsky, Gorban, Zhu, Bishop, Yamagata, Kitoh,
  Krasnoshchekov-Protopopov, Petukhov and Krasnoshchekov.

  Each of them returns the Nusselt number of the pipe; the wall
  heat-transfer coefficient follows from h = Nu*k/Di and is left to the caller.
  They take the Reynolds number Re [-], the Prandtl number Pr [-] and the
  property and temperature values the correction factors of the original
  correlation need: densities rho_w and rho_b [kg/m^3], viscosities mu_w and
  mu_b [Pa-s], conductivities k_w and k_b [W/m-K], mean and bulk heat
  capacities Cp_avg and Cp_b [J/kg-K], the bulk, wall and pseudo-critical
  temperatures T_b, T_w and T_pc [K], the enthalpy H_b of water [J/kg], the
  mass flux G [kg/m^2-s], the heat flux to the wall q [W/m^2] and, for
  Bishop, the tube diameter Di [m] and the axial distance x [m]. The
  correlations themselves handle the pressure and the position of the wall
  temperature with respect to the pseudo-critical point; no property function
  is called inside a function, the calling model passes the values (rule 5 of
  sources/ht/README.md, section 7).

  Correlations whose original form has optional corrections (the property
  ratios, and the regime-dependent exponents of Jackson, Yamagata and
  Krasnoshchekov) take a last integer argument corr: corr = 1 applies the
  corrections, corr = 0 returns the bare correlation. EES has no optional
  arguments, and the non-smooth regime switches (the enthalpy branches of Griem
  and Kitoh, the temperature branches of Jackson, Yamagata and
  Krasnoshchekov) are nested block IF statements.

  The demonstration program at the end calls every function: case "A" with the
  input set of the ht doctest of the correlation (the published values) and case
  "B" with a second, uniform input set, each time with and without the
  corrections; a third case "C" calls the branches of the non-smooth switches
  that the cases A and B do not take. The ht values of the three sets are
  tabulated in the README (verification table).

  Inputs : Re [-], Pr / Pr_w / Pr_pc [-], rho_w / rho_b [kg/m^3],
           mu_w / mu_b [Pa-s], k_w / k_b [W/m-K], Cp_avg / Cp_b [J/kg-K],
           T_b / T_w / T_pc [K], H_b [J/kg], G [kg/m^2-s], q [W/m^2],
           Di / x [m], corr [-]
  Outputs: Nu [-] (one FUNCTION per correlation)
  Units  : SI - dimensionless numbers, densities in kg/m^3, viscosities in
           Pa-s, conductivities in W/m-K, heat capacities in J/kg-K,
           temperatures in K, enthalpy in J/kg, fluxes in kg/m^2-s and W/m^2
  Source : ht (Caleb Bell), ht/conv_supercritical.py, version 1.2.0,
           commit 85e0ee6, MIT - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht ; correlations
           by the authors cited in each function block - see README.md
  Status : VERIFIED against the doctests and the values of ht 1.2.0
           (README.md, "Verification")
=============================================================================}

FUNCTION Nu_McAdams(Re, Pr)
{NU_MCADAMS - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions. ht notes that this correlation fits the enhanced
 heat transfer regime and that this form was also one of the Dittus-Boelter
 correlations; it is claimed to fit data for high pressures and low heat fluxes.

   Nu = 0.0243*Re^0.8*Pr^0.4

 Validity: no range is given in the original (as in ht).
 Original: McAdams, W. H., Heat Transmission, McGraw-Hill, New York and
   London, 1942; as given in Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su, "An
   Assessment of Correlations of Forced Convection Heat Transfer to Water at
   Supercritical Pressure", Annals of Nuclear Energy 76 (February 2015):
   451-460, doi:10.1016/j.anucene.2014.10.027.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_McAdams,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_McAdams = 0.0243*Re^0.8*Pr^0.4
END

FUNCTION Nu_Shitsman(Re, Pr_b, Pr_w)
{NU_SHITSMAN - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions.

   Nu = 0.023*Re^0.8*min(Pr_b, Pr_w)^0.8

 Re_b is the Reynolds number with bulk fluid properties, Pr_b the bulk and
 Pr_w the wall Prandtl number. The source correlation was developed with
 D = 7.8 and 8.2 mm and a Pr of approximately 1 (as in ht).
 Validity: no range is given in the original (as in ht).
 Original: Shitsman, M. E., "Impairment of the heat transmission at
   supercritical pressures", High Temperature 1(2): 237-244, 1963;
   Miropol'skii, Z. L. and Shitsman, M. E., "Heat transfer to water and steam
   at variable specific heat", J. Tech. Phys. XXVII(10): 2359-2372, 1957; as
   given in Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Shitsman,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_Shitsman = 0.023*Re^0.8*MIN(Pr_b, Pr_w)^0.8
END

FUNCTION Nu_Griem(Re, Pr, H_b, corr)
{NU_GRIEM - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions, with the fluid properties to be evaluated as
 described below.

   Nu = 0.0169*Re^0.8356*Pr_sel^0.432*w
   w  = 0.82                        if H_b < 1540 kJ/kg
   w  = 0.82 + 9E-7*(H_b - 1.54E6)  if 1540 <= H_b <= 1740 kJ/kg [J/kg]
   w  = 1                           if H_b > 1740 kJ/kg
   w  = 1                           if corr = 0

 H_b [J/kg] is the enthalpy of water; the enthalpy reference is the one of the
 1967 IFC formulation, as in the original. Property evaluation rules of the
 original: the heat capacity is calculated at five points - T_w, (T_w+T_f)/2,
 T_f, (T_b+T_f)/2, T_b - and the lowest three are averaged; the viscosity is
 the bulk viscosity; the thermal conductivity is the average of the bulk and
 wall values; the density is the bulk density.
 Validity: developed with D = 10, 14 and 20 mm, P from 22 to 27 MPa,
   G from 300 to 2500 kg/m^2/s and q from 200 to 700 kW/m^2 (as in ht).
 Original: Griem, H., "A New Procedure for the Prediction of Forced Convection
   Heat Transfer at near- and Supercritical Pressure", Heat and Mass Transfer
   31(5): 301-305, 1996, doi:10.1007/BF02184042; as given in Chen, W.,
   X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Griem,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  w = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    IF H_b < 1.54E6 THEN
      w = 0.82
    ELSE
      IF H_b > 1.74E6 THEN
        w = 1
      ELSE
        w = 0.82 + 9E-7*(H_b - 1.54E6)
      ENDIF
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
  Nu_Griem = 0.0169*Re^0.8356*Pr^0.432*w
END

FUNCTION Nu_Jackson(Re, Pr, rho_w, rho_b, Cp_avg, Cp_b, T_b, T_w, T_pc, corr)
{NU_JACKSON - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions (also called the Jackson-Hall correlation).

   Nu = 0.0183*Re^0.82*Pr^0.5*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.3*(Cp_avg/Cp_b)^n
   n = 0.4                                          if T_b < T_w < T_pc
                                                    or 1.2*T_pc < T_b < T_w
   n = 0.4 + 0.2*(T_w/T_pc - 1)                     if T_b < T_pc < T_w
   n = 0.4 + 0.2*(T_w/T_pc - 1)*[1 - 5*(T_b/T_pc - 1)]   otherwise
   n = 0.4 and no correction                        if corr = 0
   Cp_avg = (H_w - H_b)/(T_w - T_b)

 T_b, T_w and T_pc [K] are the bulk, wall and pseudo-critical temperatures,
 T_pc being the temperature at which the heat capacity is maximum at the
 pressure of the fluid; corr = 1 applies the density and heat-capacity
 corrections and the temperature-dependent exponent, corr = 0 returns the bare
 correlation.
 Validity: P from 23.4 to 29.3 MPa; G from 700 to 3600 kg/m^2/s;
   q from 46 to 2600 kW/m^2; Re from 8E4 to 5E5; D from 1.6 to 20 mm.
 Original: Jackson, J. D., "Consideration of the Heat Transfer Properties of
   Supercritical Pressure Water in Connection with the Cooling of Advanced
   Nuclear Reactors", 2002; as given in Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su
   (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Jackson,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    IF T_b < T_w AND T_w < T_pc THEN
      n = 0.4
    ELSE
      IF T_b > 1.2*T_pc AND T_b < T_w THEN
        n = 0.4
      ELSE
        IF T_b < T_pc AND T_pc < T_w THEN
          n = 0.4 + 0.2*(T_w/T_pc - 1)
        ELSE
          n = 0.4 + 0.2*(T_w/T_pc - 1)*(1 - 5*(T_b/T_pc - 1))
        ENDIF
      ENDIF
    ENDIF
    F = (rho_w/rho_b)^0.3*(Cp_avg/Cp_b)^n
  ENDIF
  Nu_Jackson = 0.0183*Re^0.82*Pr^0.5*F
END

FUNCTION Nu_Gupta(Re, Pr, rho_w, rho_b, mu_w, mu_b, corr)
{NU_GUPTA - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions, with the wall properties.

   Nu_w = 0.004*Re_w^0.923*Pr_bar_w^0.773*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.186*(mu_w/mu_b)^0.366
   Pr_bar_w uses the heat capacity averaged between the wall and the bulk
   temperatures, Cp_avg = (H_w - H_b)/(T_w - T_b)
   corr = 0 returns the bare correlation (0.004*Re^0.923*Pr^0.773)

 Validity: P = 24 MPa and D = 10 mm; G from 200 to 1500 kg/m^2/s and
   q from 0 to 1250 kW/m^2 (as in ht).
 Original: Gupta, S., A. Farah, K. King, S. Mokry and I. Pioro, "Developing
   New Heat-Transfer Correlation for SuperCritical-Water Flow in Vertical Bare
   Tubes", ICONE18-30024, 2010, doi:10.1115/ICONE18-30024; as given in
   Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Gupta,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    F = (rho_w/rho_b)^0.186*(mu_w/mu_b)^0.366
  ENDIF
  Nu_Gupta = 0.004*Re^0.923*Pr^0.773*F
END

FUNCTION Nu_Swenson(Re, Pr, rho_w, rho_b, corr)
{NU_SWENSON - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions, with the wall properties.

   Nu_w = 0.00459*Re_w^0.923*Pr_bar_w^0.613*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.231
   Pr_bar_w uses the heat capacity averaged between the wall and the bulk
   temperatures, Cp_avg = (H_w - H_b)/(T_w - T_b)
   corr = 0 returns the bare correlation

 Validity: P from 22.8 to 27.6 MPa; G from 542 to 2150 kg/m^2/s;
   Re from 7.5E4 to 3.16E6; T_b from 75 to 576 degC and T_w from 93 to
   649 degC (as in ht).
 Original: Swenson, H. S., J. R. Carver and C. R. Kakarala, "Heat Transfer to
   Supercritical Water in Smooth-Bore Tubes", Journal of Heat Transfer 87(4):
   477-483, 1 November 1965, doi:10.1115/1.3689139; as given in Chen, W.,
   X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Swenson,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    F = (rho_w/rho_b)^0.231
  ENDIF
  Nu_Swenson = 0.00459*Re^0.923*Pr^0.613*F
END

FUNCTION Nu_Xu(Re, Pr, rho_w, rho_b, mu_w, mu_b, corr)
{NU_XU - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions.

   Nu_b = 0.02269*Re_b^0.8079*Pr_bar_b^0.9213*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.6638
                                       *(mu_w/mu_b)^0.8687
   Pr_bar_b uses the heat capacity averaged between the wall and the bulk
   temperatures, Cp_avg = (H_w - H_b)/(T_w - T_b)
   corr = 0 returns the bare correlation

 Validity: P from 23 to 30 MPa and D = 12 mm; G from 600 to 1200 kg/m^2/s and
   q from 100 to 600 kW/m^2 (as in ht).
 Original: Xu, F., L. J. Guo, Y. F. Mao and X. E. Jiang, "Experimental
   investigation to the heat transfer characteristics of water in vertical
   pipes under supercritical pressure", Journal of Xi'an Jiaotong University
   39: 468-471, 2005; as given in Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015),
   above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Xu,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    F = (rho_w/rho_b)^0.6638*(mu_w/mu_b)^0.8687
  ENDIF
  Nu_Xu = 0.02269*Re^0.8079*Pr^0.9213*F
END

FUNCTION Nu_Mokry(Re, Pr, rho_w, rho_b, corr)
{NU_MOKRY - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions.

   Nu_b = 0.0061*Re_b^0.904*Pr_bar_b^0.684*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.564
   Pr_bar_b uses the heat capacity averaged between the wall and the bulk
   temperatures, Cp_avg = (H_w - H_b)/(T_w - T_b)
   corr = 0 returns the bare correlation

 Validity: P = 20 MPa and D = 10 mm; G from 200 to 1500 kg/m^2/s and
   q from 0 to 1250 kW/m^2 (as in ht).
 Original: Mokry, S., I. Pioro, A. Farah, K. King, S. Gupta, W. Peiman and
   P. Kirillov, "Development of Supercritical Water Heat-Transfer Correlation
   for Vertical Bare Tubes", Nuclear Engineering and Design 241(4): 1126-1136,
   April 2011, doi:10.1016/j.nucengdes.2010.06.012; as given in Chen, W.,
   X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Mokry,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    F = (rho_w/rho_b)^0.564
  ENDIF
  Nu_Mokry = 0.0061*Re^0.904*Pr^0.684*F
END

FUNCTION Nu_Bringer_Smith(Re, Pr)
{NU_BRINGER_SMITH - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe
 under near-supercritical conditions.

   Nu_x = 0.0266*Re_x^0.77*Pr_w^0.55

 Re_x uses the fluid properties at T_ref and Pr_w the wall Prandtl number;
 T_ref = T_b if (T_pc - T_b)/(T_w - T_b) < 0, T_pc if 0 < that ratio < 1,
 T_w if it is larger than 1 (as in the original).
 Validity: fit to data somewhat distant from the critical and pseudo-critical
   regions (as in ht); no numerical range is given.
 Original: Bringer, R. P. and J. M. Smith, "Heat Transfer in the Critical
   Region", AIChE Journal 3(1): 49-55, 1 March 1957, doi:10.1002/aic.690030110;
   as given in Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py,
   Nu_Bringer_Smith, MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_Bringer_Smith = 0.0266*Re^0.77*Pr^0.55
END

FUNCTION Nu_Ornatsky(Re, Pr_b, Pr_w, rho_w, rho_b, corr)
{NU_ORNATSKY - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions.

   Nu_b = 0.023*Re_b^0.8*min(Pr_b, Pr_w)^0.8*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.3
   corr = 0 returns the bare correlation

 Re_b is the Reynolds number with bulk fluid properties, Pr_b the bulk and
 Pr_w the wall Prandtl number.
 Validity: no range is given in the original (as in ht).
 Original: Ornatsky, A. P., L. P. Glushchenko and E. T. Siomin, "The research
   of temperature conditions of small diameter parallel tubes cooled by water
   under supercritical pressures", Proceedings of the 4th International Heat
   Transfer Conference, Paris-Versailles, vol. VI, paper B, Elsevier, 8 November
   1970; as given in Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Ornatsky,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    F = (rho_w/rho_b)^0.3
  ENDIF
  Nu_Ornatsky = 0.023*Re^0.8*MIN(Pr_b, Pr_w)^0.8*F
END

FUNCTION Nu_Gorban(Re, Pr)
{NU_GORBAN - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions. ht does not recommend this correlation.

   Nu_b = 0.0059*Re_b^0.90*Pr_b^(-0.12)

 Validity: no range is given in the original (as in ht).
 Original: Gorban, L. M., R. S. Pomet'ko and O. A. Khryaschev, "Modeling of
   water heat transfer with Freon of supercritical pressure", report 1766,
   Institute of Physics and Power Engineering, Obninsk, 1990; as given in
   Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Gorban,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_Gorban = 0.0059*Re^0.90*Pr^(-0.12)
END

FUNCTION Nu_Zhu(Re, Pr, rho_w, rho_b, k_w, k_b, corr)
{NU_ZHU - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions.

   Nu_b = 0.0068*Re_b^0.9*Pr_bar_b^0.63*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.17*(k_w/k_b)^0.29
   Pr_bar_b uses the heat capacity averaged between the wall and the bulk
   temperatures, Cp_avg = (H_w - H_b)/(T_w - T_b)
   corr = 0 returns the bare correlation

 Validity: P from 22 to 30 MPa and D = 26 mm; G from 600 to 1200 kg/m^2/s and
   q from 200 to 600 kW/m^2 (as in ht).
 Original: Zhu, X., Q. Bi, D. Yang and T. Chen, "An Investigation on Heat
   Transfer Characteristics of Different Pressure Steam-Water in Vertical
   Upward Tube", Nuclear Engineering and Design 239(2): 381-388, February 2009,
   doi:10.1016/j.nucengdes.2008.10.026; as given in Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu
   and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Zhu,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    F = (rho_w/rho_b)^0.17*(k_w/k_b)^0.29
  ENDIF
  Nu_Zhu = 0.0068*Re^0.9*Pr^0.63*F
END

FUNCTION Nu_Bishop(Re, Pr, rho_w, rho_b, Di, x, corr)
{NU_BISHOP - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions, with the adjustment of the thermal entry length.
 ht calls it one of the most common correlations for supercritical convection.

   Nu_b = 0.0069*Re_b^0.9*Pr_bar_b^0.66*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.43*(1 + 2.4*Di/x)
   Pr_bar_b uses the heat capacity averaged between the wall and the bulk
   temperatures, Cp_avg = (H_w - H_b)/(T_w - T_b)
   corr = 0 returns the bare correlation without the entry correction

 Di [m] is the tube diameter and x [m] the axial distance along the tube.
 Validity: P from 22.8 to 27.6 MPa; x/D from 30 to 365; G from 651 to
   3662 kg/m^2/s and q from 310 to 3460 kW/m^2; T_b from 282 to 527 degC.
 Original: Bishop, A. A., R. O. Sandberg and L. S. Tong, "Forced convection
   heat transfer to water at near-critical temperature and supercritical
   pressures", AIChE Chemical Engineering Symposium Series no. 2, Institute of
   Chemical Engineers, London, 1965; as given in Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and
   X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Bishop,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    F = (rho_w/rho_b)^0.43*(1 + 2.4*Di/x)
  ENDIF
  Nu_Bishop = 0.0069*Re^0.9*Pr^0.66*F
END

FUNCTION Nu_Yamagata(Re, Pr, Pr_pc, Cp_avg, Cp_b, T_b, T_w, T_pc, corr)
{NU_YAMAGATA - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions.

   Nu_b = 0.0138*Re_b^0.85*Pr_b^0.8*F
   E = (T_pc - T_b)/(T_w - T_b)
   F = (Cp_avg/Cp_b)^n2                                    if E < 0
   F = 0.67*Pr_pc^(-0.05)*(Cp_avg/Cp_b)^n1                 if 0 <= E < 1
   F = 1                                                   if E >= 1
   n1 = -0.77*(1 + 1/Pr_pc) + 1.49
   n2 =  1.44*(1 + 1/Pr_pc) - 0.53
   F = 1 and no correction                                if corr = 0

 Pr_pc [-] is the Prandtl number at the pseudo-critical temperature, Cp_avg
 [J/kg-K] the heat capacity averaged between the wall and the bulk
 temperatures, Cp_b [J/kg-K] the heat capacity at the bulk temperature;
 T_b, T_w and T_pc [K] are the bulk, wall and pseudo-critical temperatures.
 Validity: P from 22.6 to 29.4 MPa; D = 7.5 and 10 mm; G from 310 to
   1830 kg/m^2/s and q from 116 to 930 kW/m^2; T_b from 230 to 540 degC.
 Original: Yamagata, K., K. Nishikawa, S. Hasegawa, T. Fujii and S. Yoshida,
   "Forced Convective Heat Transfer to Supercritical Water Flowing in Tubes",
   International Journal of Heat and Mass Transfer 15(12): 2575-2593,
   1 December 1972, doi:10.1016/0017-9310(72)90148-2; as given in Chen, W.,
   X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Yamagata,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    E = (T_pc - T_b)/(T_w - T_b)
    IF E < 0 THEN
      n2 = 1.44*(1 + 1/Pr_pc) - 0.53
      F = (Cp_avg/Cp_b)^n2
    ELSE
      IF E < 1 THEN
        n1 = -0.77*(1 + 1/Pr_pc) + 1.49
        F = 0.67*Pr_pc^(-0.05)*(Cp_avg/Cp_b)^n1
      ENDIF
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
  Nu_Yamagata = 0.0138*Re^0.85*Pr^0.8*F
END

FUNCTION Nu_Kitoh(Re, Pr, H_b, G, q, corr)
{NU_KITOH - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions, with the exponent corrected by the fluid enthalpy,
 the mass flux and the heat flux.

   Nu_b = 0.015*Re_b^0.85*Pr_b^m
   q_dht = 200*G^1.2
   m = 0.69 - 81000/q_dht + f_c*q
   f_c =  2.9E-8 + 0.11/q_dht       if H_b < 1500 kJ/kg
   f_c = -8.7E-8 - 0.65/q_dht       if 1500 <= H_b <= 3300 kJ/kg
   f_c = -9.7E-7 +  1.3/q_dht       if H_b > 3300 kJ/kg
   m = 0.69                         if corr = 0

 H_b [J/kg] is the enthalpy of water; its reference point is not stated in the
 original (as in ht). G [kg/m^2/s] is the mass flux of the fluid and q [W/m^2]
 the heat flux to the wall. The upper and lower enthalpy limits of the
 correlation are 4000 kJ/kg and 0 kJ/kg but are not enforced here.
 Validity: G from 100 to 1750 kg/m^2/s, q from 0 to 1800 kW/m^2 and T_b from
   20 to 550 degC (as in ht); ht notes that the correlation does not behave
   realistically outside these and should not be used there.
 Original: Kitoh, K., S. Koshizuka and Y. Oka, "Refinement of Transient
   Criteria and Safety Analysis for a High-Temperature Reactor Cooled by
   Supercritical Water", Nuclear Technology 135(3): 252-264,
   1 September 2001; as given in Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015),
   above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Kitoh,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  m = 0.69
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    q_dht = 200*G^1.2
    IF H_b < 1.5E6 THEN
      f_c = 2.9E-8 + 0.11/q_dht
    ELSE
      IF H_b <= 3.3E6 THEN
        f_c = -8.7E-8 - 0.65/q_dht
      ELSE
        f_c = -9.7E-7 + 1.3/q_dht
      ENDIF
    ENDIF
    m = 0.69 - 81000/q_dht + f_c*q
  ENDIF
  Nu_Kitoh = 0.015*Re^0.85*Pr^m
END

FUNCTION Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov(Re, Pr, Cp_avg, Cp_b, k_w, k_b, mu_w, mu_b, corr)
{NU_KRASNOSHCHEKOV_PROTOPOPOV - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow
 in a pipe under supercritical conditions, with the viscosity, thermal
 conductivity and heat-capacity corrections.

   Nu_0 = (fd/8)*Re*Pr_bar / {1.07 + 12.7*(fd/8)^0.5*(Pr_bar^(2/3) - 1)}
   fd  = [1.82*LOG10(Re) - 1.64]^(-2)
   Nu  = Nu_0*(mu_w/mu_b)^0.11*(k_w/k_b)^(-0.33)*(Cp_avg/Cp_b)^0.35
   corr = 0 returns Nu_0

 Validity: P from 22.3 to 32 MPa; Re from 2E4 to 8.6E6; Pr from 0.86 to 86;
   viscosity ratio from 0.9 to 3.6, thermal conductivity ratio from 1 to 6
   and heat capacity ratio from 0.07 to 4.5 (as in ht).
 Original: Krasnoshchekov, E. A. and Protopopov, V. S., "Heat transfer at
   supercritical region in flow of carbon dioxide and water in tubes",
   Therm. Eng. 12: 26-30, 1959; as given in Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and
   X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py,
   Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov, MIT License, C. Bell -
   https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  fd = (1.82*LOG10(Re) - 1.64)^(-2)
  Nu_0 = (fd/8)*Re*Pr/(1.07 + 12.7*(fd/8)^0.5*(Pr^(2/3) - 1))
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    F = (mu_w/mu_b)^0.11*(k_w/k_b)^(-0.33)*(Cp_avg/Cp_b)^0.35
  ENDIF
  Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov = Nu_0*F
END

FUNCTION Nu_Petukhov(Re, Pr, rho_w, rho_b, mu_w, mu_b, corr)
{NU_PETUKHOV - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe under
 supercritical conditions, with the friction factor corrected by the density
 and viscosity ratios.

   Nu_b = (f/8)*Re*Pr_bar / {1 + 900/Re + 12.7*(f/8)^0.5*(Pr_bar^(2/3) - 1)}
   f   = fd*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.4*(mu_w/mu_b)^0.2
   fd  = [1.82*LOG10(Re) - 1.64]^(-2)
   corr = 0 returns the bare correlation with fd

 Validity: no range is given in the original (as in ht).
 Original: Petukhov, B. S., V. A. Kurganov and V. B. Ankudinov, "Heat Transfer
   and Flow Resistance in the Turbulent Pipe Flow of a Fluid with Near-Critical
   State Parameters", High Temperature 21(1): 81-89, 1983; as given in
   Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py, Nu_Petukhov,
   MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  fd = (1.82*LOG10(Re) - 1.64)^(-2)
  f = fd
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    f = fd*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.4*(mu_w/mu_b)^0.2
  ENDIF
  Nu_Petukhov = (f/8)*Re*Pr/(1 + 900/Re + 12.7*(f/8)^0.5*(Pr^(2/3) - 1))
END

FUNCTION Nu_Krasnoshchekov(Re, Pr, rho_w, rho_b, Cp_avg, Cp_b, T_b, T_w, T_pc, corr)
{NU_KRASNOSHCHEKOV - Nusselt number, turbulent vertical upward flow in a pipe
 under supercritical conditions, with the density correction and the
 temperature-dependent heat-capacity exponent.

   Nu_0 = (fd/8)*Re*Pr_bar / {1.07 + 12.7*(fd/8)^0.5*(Pr_bar^(2/3) - 1)}
   fd  = [1.82*LOG10(Re) - 1.64]^(-2)
   Nu  = Nu_0*(rho_w/rho_b)^0.3*(Cp_avg/Cp_b)^n
   n1  = 0.22 + 0.18*T_w/T_pc
   n = 0.4                          if T_b < T_w < T_pc
                                     or 1.2*T_pc < T_b < T_w
   n = n1                           if 1 < T_w/T_pc < 2.5
   n = n1 + (5*n1 - 2)*(1 - T_b/T_pc)   otherwise
   n = 0.4 and no correction        if corr = 0

 T_b, T_w and T_pc [K] are the bulk, wall and pseudo-critical temperatures;
 Cp_avg [J/kg-K] is the heat capacity averaged between the wall and the bulk
 temperatures and Cp_b [J/kg-K] the heat capacity at the bulk temperature.
 Validity: P from 23.4 to 29.3 MPa; G from 700 to 3600 kg/m^2/s;
   q from 46 to 2600 kW/m^2; Re from 8E4 to 5E5; D from 1.6 to 20 mm.
 Original: Krasnoshchekov, E. A., Protopopov, V. S. and Kuraeva, I. V.,
   "Experimental investigation of heat transfer for carbon dioxide in the
   supercritical region", Proceedings of the Second All-Soviet Union
   Conference on Heat and Mass Transfer, Minsk, May 1967; as given in
   Chen, W., X. Fang, Y. Xu and X. Su (2015), above.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_supercritical.py,
   Nu_Krasnoshchekov, MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  n = 0.4
  F = 1
  IF corr = 1 THEN
    n1 = 0.22 + 0.18*T_w/T_pc
    IF T_b < T_w AND T_w < T_pc THEN
      n = 0.4
    ELSE
      IF T_b > 1.2*T_pc AND T_b < T_w THEN
        n = 0.4
      ELSE
        IF T_w/T_pc > 1 AND T_w/T_pc < 2.5 THEN
          n = n1
        ELSE
          n = n1 + (5*n1 - 2)*(1 - T_b/T_pc)
        ENDIF
      ENDIF
    ENDIF
    F = (rho_w/rho_b)^0.3*(Cp_avg/Cp_b)^n
  ENDIF
  fd = (1.82*LOG10(Re) - 1.64)^(-2)
  Nu_0 = (fd/8)*Re*Pr/(1.07 + 12.7*(fd/8)^0.5*(Pr^(2/3) - 1))
  Nu_Krasnoshchekov = Nu_0*F
END

"!Demonstration program: every correlation is called twice per input set"
"It is ignored by the future $INCLUDE library:supercritical_internal_nu (CS-FEAT-IMPORT)."
"Case A: the input set of the ht doctest of each correlation (published values)."
"Case B: one second, uniform input set (Re = 3E5, Pr = 2.5, T_b = 390 K,"
"        T_w = 420 K, T_pc = 400 K, G = 1400 kg/m^2-s, q = 650 kW/m^2)."
"Each function is called without (corr = 0) and with (corr = 1) its optional"
"corrections. Both cases are tabulated against ht in the README."

"Case A - input set (ht doctests)"
Re_A = 1E5
Pr_A = 1.2
Pr_w_A = 1.6
Pr_pc_A = 1.5
rho_w_A = 330
rho_b_A = 290
mu_w_A = 8E-4
mu_b_A = 9E-4
k_w_A = 0.63
k_b_A = 0.69
Cp_avg_A = 2080.845
Cp_b_A = 2048.621
T_b_A = 650
T_w_A = 700
T_pc_A = 600
Di_A = 0.01
x_A = 1.2
H_b_A = 1.3E6
G_A = 1500
q_A = 5E6
"the doctest of Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov passes other heat capacities and conductivities"
k_w_P_A = 0.62
k_b_P_A = 0.52
Cp_avg_P_A = 330
Cp_b_P_A = 290

"Nu_McAdams"
Nu_McAdams_A = Nu_McAdams(Re_A, Pr_A)
"Nu_Shitsman - bulk and wall Prandtl numbers"
Nu_Shitsman_A = Nu_Shitsman(Re_A, Pr_A, Pr_w_A)
"Nu_Griem - bare form, and with the enthalpy correction"
Nu_Griem_A = Nu_Griem(Re_A, Pr_A, H_b_A, 0)
Nu_Griem_corr_A = Nu_Griem(Re_A, Pr_A, H_b_A, 1)
"Nu_Jackson - bare form, and with the density and heat-capacity corrections"
Nu_Jackson_A = Nu_Jackson(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, Cp_avg_A, Cp_b_A, T_b_A, T_w_A, T_pc_A, 0)
Nu_Jackson_corr_A = Nu_Jackson(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, Cp_avg_A, Cp_b_A, T_b_A, T_w_A, T_pc_A, 1)
"Nu_Gupta - bare form, and with the density and viscosity corrections"
Nu_Gupta_A = Nu_Gupta(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, mu_w_A, mu_b_A, 0)
Nu_Gupta_corr_A = Nu_Gupta(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, mu_w_A, mu_b_A, 1)
"Nu_Swenson - bare form, and with the density correction"
Nu_Swenson_A = Nu_Swenson(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, 0)
Nu_Swenson_corr_A = Nu_Swenson(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, 1)
"Nu_Xu - bare form, and with the density and viscosity corrections"
Nu_Xu_A = Nu_Xu(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, mu_w_A, mu_b_A, 0)
Nu_Xu_corr_A = Nu_Xu(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, mu_w_A, mu_b_A, 1)
"Nu_Mokry - bare form, and with the density correction"
Nu_Mokry_A = Nu_Mokry(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, 0)
Nu_Mokry_corr_A = Nu_Mokry(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, 1)
"Nu_Bringer_Smith"
Nu_Bringer_Smith_A = Nu_Bringer_Smith(Re_A, Pr_A)
"Nu_Ornatsky - bare form, and with the density correction"
Nu_Ornatsky_A = Nu_Ornatsky(Re_A, Pr_A, Pr_w_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, 0)
Nu_Ornatsky_corr_A = Nu_Ornatsky(Re_A, Pr_A, Pr_w_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, 1)
"Nu_Gorban"
Nu_Gorban_A = Nu_Gorban(Re_A, Pr_A)
"Nu_Zhu - bare form, and with the density and conductivity corrections"
Nu_Zhu_A = Nu_Zhu(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, k_w_A, k_b_A, 0)
Nu_Zhu_corr_A = Nu_Zhu(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, k_w_A, k_b_A, 1)
"Nu_Bishop - bare form, and with the density and entry-length corrections"
Nu_Bishop_A = Nu_Bishop(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, Di_A, x_A, 0)
Nu_Bishop_corr_A = Nu_Bishop(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, Di_A, x_A, 1)
"Nu_Yamagata - bare form, and with the heat-capacity correction"
Nu_Yamagata_A = Nu_Yamagata(Re_A, Pr_A, Pr_pc_A, Cp_avg_A, Cp_b_A, T_b_A, T_w_A, T_pc_A, 0)
Nu_Yamagata_corr_A = Nu_Yamagata(Re_A, Pr_A, Pr_pc_A, Cp_avg_A, Cp_b_A, T_b_A, T_w_A, T_pc_A, 1)
"Nu_Kitoh - bare form, and with the enthalpy, mass-flux and heat-flux correction"
Nu_Kitoh_A = Nu_Kitoh(Re_A, Pr_A, H_b_A, G_A, q_A, 0)
Nu_Kitoh_corr_A = Nu_Kitoh(Re_A, Pr_A, H_b_A, G_A, q_A, 1)
"Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov - bare form, and with the viscosity, conductivity and heat-capacity corrections"
Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov_A = Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov(Re_A, Pr_A, Cp_avg_P_A, Cp_b_P_A, k_w_P_A, k_b_P_A, mu_w_A, mu_b_A, 0)
Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov_corr_A = Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov(Re_A, Pr_A, Cp_avg_P_A, Cp_b_P_A, k_w_P_A, k_b_P_A, mu_w_A, mu_b_A, 1)
"Nu_Petukhov - bare form, and with the density and viscosity corrections"
Nu_Petukhov_A = Nu_Petukhov(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, mu_w_A, mu_b_A, 0)
Nu_Petukhov_corr_A = Nu_Petukhov(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, mu_w_A, mu_b_A, 1)
"Nu_Krasnoshchekov - bare form, and with the density and heat-capacity corrections"
Nu_Krasnoshchekov_A = Nu_Krasnoshchekov(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, Cp_avg_A, Cp_b_A, T_b_A, T_w_A, T_pc_A, 0)
Nu_Krasnoshchekov_corr_A = Nu_Krasnoshchekov(Re_A, Pr_A, rho_w_A, rho_b_A, Cp_avg_A, Cp_b_A, T_b_A, T_w_A, T_pc_A, 1)

"Case B - second, uniform input set"
Re_B = 3E5
Pr_B = 2.5
Pr_w_B = 4
Pr_pc_B = 2.2
rho_w_B = 420
rho_b_B = 280
mu_w_B = 5.5E-5
mu_b_B = 7.5E-5
k_w_B = 0.095
k_b_B = 0.078
Cp_avg_B = 5200
Cp_b_B = 4100
T_b_B = 390
T_w_B = 420
T_pc_B = 400
Di_B = 0.016
x_B = 2
H_b_B = 1.6E6
G_B = 1400
q_B = 650E3
k_w_P_B = 0.088
k_b_P_B = 0.092
Cp_avg_P_B = 6100
Cp_b_P_B = 4600

"Nu_McAdams"
Nu_McAdams_B = Nu_McAdams(Re_B, Pr_B)
"Nu_Shitsman - bulk and wall Prandtl numbers"
Nu_Shitsman_B = Nu_Shitsman(Re_B, Pr_B, Pr_w_B)
"Nu_Griem - bare form, and with the enthalpy correction"
Nu_Griem_B = Nu_Griem(Re_B, Pr_B, H_b_B, 0)
Nu_Griem_corr_B = Nu_Griem(Re_B, Pr_B, H_b_B, 1)
"Nu_Jackson - bare form, and with the density and heat-capacity corrections"
Nu_Jackson_B = Nu_Jackson(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, T_b_B, T_w_B, T_pc_B, 0)
Nu_Jackson_corr_B = Nu_Jackson(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, T_b_B, T_w_B, T_pc_B, 1)
"Nu_Gupta - bare form, and with the density and viscosity corrections"
Nu_Gupta_B = Nu_Gupta(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, mu_w_B, mu_b_B, 0)
Nu_Gupta_corr_B = Nu_Gupta(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, mu_w_B, mu_b_B, 1)
"Nu_Swenson - bare form, and with the density correction"
Nu_Swenson_B = Nu_Swenson(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, 0)
Nu_Swenson_corr_B = Nu_Swenson(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, 1)
"Nu_Xu - bare form, and with the density and viscosity corrections"
Nu_Xu_B = Nu_Xu(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, mu_w_B, mu_b_B, 0)
Nu_Xu_corr_B = Nu_Xu(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, mu_w_B, mu_b_B, 1)
"Nu_Mokry - bare form, and with the density correction"
Nu_Mokry_B = Nu_Mokry(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, 0)
Nu_Mokry_corr_B = Nu_Mokry(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, 1)
"Nu_Bringer_Smith"
Nu_Bringer_Smith_B = Nu_Bringer_Smith(Re_B, Pr_B)
"Nu_Ornatsky - bare form, and with the density correction"
Nu_Ornatsky_B = Nu_Ornatsky(Re_B, Pr_B, Pr_w_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, 0)
Nu_Ornatsky_corr_B = Nu_Ornatsky(Re_B, Pr_B, Pr_w_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, 1)
"Nu_Gorban"
Nu_Gorban_B = Nu_Gorban(Re_B, Pr_B)
"Nu_Zhu - bare form, and with the density and conductivity corrections"
Nu_Zhu_B = Nu_Zhu(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, k_w_B, k_b_B, 0)
Nu_Zhu_corr_B = Nu_Zhu(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, k_w_B, k_b_B, 1)
"Nu_Bishop - bare form, and with the density and entry-length corrections"
Nu_Bishop_B = Nu_Bishop(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, Di_B, x_B, 0)
Nu_Bishop_corr_B = Nu_Bishop(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, Di_B, x_B, 1)
"Nu_Yamagata - bare form, and with the heat-capacity correction"
Nu_Yamagata_B = Nu_Yamagata(Re_B, Pr_B, Pr_pc_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, T_b_B, T_w_B, T_pc_B, 0)
Nu_Yamagata_corr_B = Nu_Yamagata(Re_B, Pr_B, Pr_pc_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, T_b_B, T_w_B, T_pc_B, 1)
"Nu_Kitoh - bare form, and with the enthalpy, mass-flux and heat-flux correction"
Nu_Kitoh_B = Nu_Kitoh(Re_B, Pr_B, H_b_B, G_B, q_B, 0)
Nu_Kitoh_corr_B = Nu_Kitoh(Re_B, Pr_B, H_b_B, G_B, q_B, 1)
"Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov - bare form, and with the viscosity, conductivity and heat-capacity corrections"
Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov_B = Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov(Re_B, Pr_B, Cp_avg_P_B, Cp_b_P_B, k_w_P_B, k_b_P_B, mu_w_B, mu_b_B, 0)
Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov_corr_B = Nu_Krasnoshchekov_Protopopov(Re_B, Pr_B, Cp_avg_P_B, Cp_b_P_B, k_w_P_B, k_b_P_B, mu_w_B, mu_b_B, 1)
"Nu_Petukhov - bare form, and with the density and viscosity corrections"
Nu_Petukhov_B = Nu_Petukhov(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, mu_w_B, mu_b_B, 0)
Nu_Petukhov_corr_B = Nu_Petukhov(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, mu_w_B, mu_b_B, 1)
"Nu_Krasnoshchekov - bare form, and with the density and heat-capacity corrections"
Nu_Krasnoshchekov_B = Nu_Krasnoshchekov(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, T_b_B, T_w_B, T_pc_B, 0)
Nu_Krasnoshchekov_corr_B = Nu_Krasnoshchekov(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, T_b_B, T_w_B, T_pc_B, 1)
"!Case C - the branches of the non-smooth switches not taken by the cases A and B"
"Only the argument that picks the branch changes (a temperature or the enthalpy of"
"the water); every other argument is the one of case B. The section titles give the"
"branch of the original, as written in the comment block of the function."
"Nu_Griem - the lower enthalpy branch, w = 0.82 (H_b < 1540 kJ/kg)"
Nu_Griem_w1_C = Nu_Griem(Re_B, Pr_B, 1.2E6, 1)
"Nu_Jackson - the branch n = 0.4 for T_b < T_w < T_pc"
Nu_Jackson_n1_C = Nu_Jackson(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, 380, 390, 400, 1)
"Nu_Jackson - the branch n = 0.4 for 1.2*T_pc < T_b < T_w"
Nu_Jackson_n2_C = Nu_Jackson(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, 500, 600, 400, 1)
"Nu_Krasnoshchekov - the branch n = 0.4 for T_b < T_w < T_pc"
Nu_Krasnoshchekov_n0p4_C = Nu_Krasnoshchekov(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, 380, 390, 400, 1)
"Nu_Krasnoshchekov - the last branch n = n1 + (5*n1-2)*(1-T_b/T_pc) for T_w/T_pc > 2.5"
Nu_Krasnoshchekov_n3_C = Nu_Krasnoshchekov(Re_B, Pr_B, rho_w_B, rho_b_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, 380, 1100, 400, 1)
"Nu_Yamagata - the branch F = 1 for (T_pc-T_b)/(T_w-T_b) >= 1"
Nu_Yamagata_F1_C = Nu_Yamagata(Re_B, Pr_B, Pr_pc_B, Cp_avg_B, Cp_b_B, 380, 385, 400, 1)
"Nu_Kitoh - the upper enthalpy branch of f_c (H_b > 3300 kJ/kg)"
Nu_Kitoh_fc3_C = Nu_Kitoh(Re_B, Pr_B, 3.6E6, G_B, q_B, 1)
